1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,680
Singapore's critical infrastructure boards must now own cyber recovery, in writing.

2
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:09,030
Here's what that means for your document archive.

3
00:00:09,530 --> 00:00:18,310
On 22 July 2026, the Cyber Security Agency set a new standard for boards of critical infrastructure operators across all 11 sectors.

4
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:28,440
Each board needs a documented cyber resilience framework covering risk tolerance, mitigation, transfer and recovery, reviewed at least once a year.

5
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:30,500
Two dates matter.

6
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:36,620
Auditors and service providers must comply by 31 December 2026.

7
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:41,460
Operators themselves have until 31 December 2027.

8
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:45,820
Most archives are strong on prevention and weak on recovery.

9
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:52,550
A cloud backup encrypted alongside the main system is just a second copy of the same vulnerability.

10
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:55,400
Archival microfilm closes that gap.

11
00:00:55,750 --> 00:01:02,270
LE-500 film has no network interface, so it cannot be encrypted, exfiltrated or altered remotely.

12
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:09,680
Identify your board-critical records, and keep a permanent microfilm copy alongside your digital backups.

13
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:16,370
Micrographics Data can help you scope a recovery-layer archive before the December 2026 deadline.

14
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,590
Request a quote at micrographicsdata.com.
